_node\": {\"302\": 1276, \"722\": 52, \"767\": 2, \"1635\": 9}, \"window\": [0, 100000]}", "latency_ms": 17, "tokens_before": 30041, "tokens_after": 30041, "ok": true, "error": ""}], "final_claim": {"task_id": "t_9cf28e7b", "decision": "insufficient", "mechanism": "cascade", "entities": [767, 722, 302, 1635], "window": [20674, 59073], "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_16", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "A static directed path 767->722->302->1635 exists (steps 03,07,12,13,14), with no direct 767->1635 edge (step_15). However, the only bridge is node 302, a hub with 269 distinct partners and 1276 incident triangles (steps 11,16), so static 3-hop reachability is near-generic in this dense 16k-event graph (step_01) and does not by itself indicate a cascade. Critically, every available tool aggregates over the full horizon [0,100000] and exposes no timestamps, so I could not confirm that the three single-occurrence hops (each n_events=1) fall within [20674,59073], occur in the required order, or satisfy the <=600 time-unit inter-hop delay. The temporal evidence needed to affirm or reject the cascade is unavailable, so the honest verdict is 'insufficient' rather than 'present' (which would rest only on the time-ignored connection) or 'absent'.", "confidence": 0.6}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**What I could establish:**\n- 767 is a low-activity node (9 events, 9 distinct partners); 1635 likewise (11 events) \u2014 step_02.\n- 767's out-neighbours are {722, 988, 1292, 1719}; 1635's in-neighbours are {106, 133, 302, 733} \u2014 steps 03, 04.\n- There is **no direct** 767\u21921635 edge (step_15).\n- A **static** directed path exists: 767\u2192722 (step_12), 722\u2192302 (step_07, step_13), 302\u21921635 (step_14). Each of these three hops occurred exactly **once** over the whole horizon.\n\n**Confounder controls:**\n- The bridging node 302 is a massive hub: 269 distinct partners, 344 events, 1276 incident triangles (steps 11, 16). Almost any low-degree pair in this graph is statically 3-hop connected through such a hub, so the existence of the path when time is ignored carries essentially no evidence about a cascade \u2014 exactly the alternative explanation the task warns against.\n- Similarly, 1635's other in-neighbour 733 is an even larger hub (485 partners). Global activity is high (16,325 events, repeat_ratio 0.031, step_01), which again inflates static reachability without implying temporal ordering.\n\n**The decisive gap:** every tool available to me reports over the *whole* horizon [0, 100000] and returns **no timestamps**. I could not verify (a) that the three hops fall inside [20674, 59073], (b) that they occur in the order 767\u2192722 \u2192 722\u2192302 \u2192 302\u21921635, or (c) that consecutive hops are separated by \u2264 600 time units. Since each hop is a single event, there is exactly one candidate ordering, and it has a prior probability of ~1/6 of being time-respecting at all \u2014 before even imposing the tight 600-unit gap. The evidence therefore neither confirms nor refutes the cascade.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_9cf28e7b\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [767, 722, 302, 1635],\n  \"window\": [20674, 59073],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"A static directed path 767->722->302->1635 exists (steps 03,07,12,13,14), with no direct 767->1635 edge (step_15). However, the only bridge is node 302, a hub with 269 distinct partners and 1276 incident triangles (steps 11,16), so static 3-hop reachability is near-generic in this dense 16k-event graph (step_01) and does not by itself indicate a cascade. Critically, every available tool aggregates over the full horizon [0,100000] and exposes no timestamps, so I could not confirm that the three single-occurrence hops (each n_events=1) fall within [20674,59073], occur in the required order, or satisfy the <=600 time-unit inter-hop delay. The temporal evidence needed to affirm or reject the cascade is unavailable, so the honest verdict is 'insufficient' rather than 'present' (which would rest only on the time-ignored connection) or 'absent'.\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.6\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 39035, "total_output_tokens": 4607, "total_tokens": 43642, "total_latency_ms": 65094, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}